  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\n- Rain into early Friday\n\nTropical moisture has begun to stream through the region today.\nA large upper level low is moving through the four corners region\nof southwest USA and propagating eastward. That low is advecting \ncopious amounts of Gulf moisture along a boundary between a large\nupper level low moving across northern Canada and a high pressure\nsystem residing over southeastern US. That strong gradient is \nbookmarked by a trough situated over the Mid Atlantic. This\nformation is allowing that ribbon of gulf moisture to stream \nthrough the midwest and Great Lakes region through tomorrow night.\n\nUpwards of three to four tenths has fallen across the \nLower peninsula with periods of light to moderate rain \ncontinuing. The period with the most moisture should be through \nthe first half of tomorrow. NAEFS and EC ensembles are showing \naverage PWATS upwards of 1.75 inches streaming across southern \nLower Michigan. This could correlate to additional 1 to 2 inches. \nLocally higher amounts along lakeshore upwards of 2 to 2.5 inches \nare possible. However given the dry soils, flooding is not a \nconcern at this time. The areas to could see showers overperform \nare Muskegon, Oceana and Newaygo counties.\n\n- Clear and cold with possible frost/freeze\n\nThe before mentioned upper level system through Canada will engulf\nthe low that is moving through the southwest. That will create a\ntrough that will stretch across most of the US and drive the high\npressure eastward. This will shift the moisture through the Mid \nAtlantic and through the southern US Friday. This will bring \ncooler, drier air through Michigan. Expect Autumn temperatures \nthrough the latter half of the week and through the weekend. Below\nnormal minimum temperatures are expected this weekend. Overnight \nlows could reach frost/freeze north of I 96, especially along the \nUS 10 corridor.",
